The list was built by the factory that promises it can never change. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B01 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            List.of("tea", "coffee").set(0, "juice");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

One of the values handed to the factory is missing. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B02 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            List.of("a", null, "b");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

The source list was built by a factory that allows missing values, and the copy factory is stricter. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B03 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            List<String> copy = List.copyOf(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("a", null)));
            System.out.println(copy);
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

The original list is changed after the read-only list was created. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B04 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> backing = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a"));
        List<String> readOnly = Collections.unmodifiableList(backing);
        backing.add("b");
        System.out.println(readOnly);
    }
}

The wrapper promises that callers cannot change the list it wraps. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B05 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> backing = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a"));
        List<String> readOnly = Collections.unmodifiableList(backing);
        try {
            readOnly.add("c");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

Three primitives are handed to the factory that builds a list from values. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class B06 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3);
        System.out.println(list.get(0).getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}

The list is a view onto an array, so its length is the array's length. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B07 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Arrays.asList("a", "b").add("c");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

A window of the first two elements is taken, then the original list is changed. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class B08 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> backing = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b", "c"));
        List<String> window = backing.subList(0, 2);
        backing.set(0, "z");
        System.out.println(window);
    }
}

A window of the first two elements is taken and then grown inside its own range. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class B09 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> backing = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b", "c"));
        List<String> window = backing.subList(0, 2);
        window.add("z");
        System.out.println(backing);
    }
}

The list refuses to grow, yet the rotation helper still manages to reorder it. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B10 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
        Collections.rotate(list, 1);
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

The list refuses to grow, yet the swap helper still works on it. What does the program print?

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B11 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
        Collections.swap(list, 0, 2);
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

The shared empty list is asked for twice. What does the program print?

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B12 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> first = Collections.emptyList();
        List<String> second = Collections.emptyList();
        System.out.println((first == second) + " " + first.size());
    }
}

The list is built by the factory that always produces a one-element list. What does the program print?

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

public class B13 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Collections.singletonList("x").set(0, "y");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
